WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.520
Welcome to the test set. Here we talk with some of the brightest thinkers and tinkerers

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.440
in statistical analysis, scientific computing, and machine learning. Digging into what makes

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:18.320
them tick, plus the insights, experiments, and OMG moments that shape the field.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.360
Back in 2020, if you submitted a talk for Posit's conference, you might remember having

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.240
to send in a 60 second video with your pitch. And you might think they were trying to figure

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.520
out how good a speaker you were, but in reality it was almost the opposite. They wanted to

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.240
bet on people with great ideas who could figure out the speaking part through a little bit

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.480
of training. And that speaker training bet basically rode on Ruth Milligan. Ruth is the

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.119
founder of Articulation, a curator for TEDxColumbus, and the author of The Motivated Speaker. Over

00:00:45.119 --> 00:00:49.200
the years, she and her team have coached hundreds of Posit Comp speakers through a process that

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.840
starts with a simple question. What's the one thing you want your audience to leave

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:57.480
with? Ruth has a message for data scientists. We're smart, and we're curious, and we have

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.439
way too much to say. We cover deciding what your talk is really about, how much evidence

00:01:02.439 --> 00:01:06.720
your audience needs, and Ruth even put us through a speaking exercise to cure us of

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:14.040
all our filler words, which honestly I need super bad. So with that, Ruth Milligan.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:20.680
Welcome to the test set. We're here with Ruth Milligan, a founder at Articulation and a curator

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:29.720
at TEDxColumbus, and author of the book The Motivated Speaker, and I think a really interesting

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.559
speaking coach. And I also love to use the word trainer. I feel like trainer is such

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:39.360
a neat way to describe it. Ruth, thanks so much for coming on to the test set.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.120
Thanks to all of you for having me. It's a real pleasure to be here.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.239
Yeah, and I know you and Posit go way back. I do also have to introduce us. I often forget

00:01:49.239 --> 00:01:55.040
that. So just to go the full loop, I'm the host, Michael Chow. I'm a software engineer

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:01.239
at Posit and joined by our co-host, Hadley Wickham, who is a chief scientist at Posit,

00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:08.520
and Wes McKinney, who's a principal architect at Posit and also founder at Kenio, a new

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:15.960
AI systems company. You heard it here first. Hadley, maybe you could tell us how y'all

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.520
know each other, because I think you and Ruth go way back.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:24.839
I mean, we met, I mean, I guess I reached out to a coach to get your help coaching the

00:02:24.839 --> 00:02:33.000
first, well, not PositConf, but RStudioConf. Do you remember who you learned me about from?

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.080
I feel was it like Women in Analytics Conference, Women in Data Science?

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:44.039
Yeah, one of your associates was the speaker. And I remember just thinking, like, what a

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:50.199
brilliant idea that was to actually give people who speak at your conference training and

00:02:50.199 --> 00:02:59.679
giving talks. Because as a veteran of academic conferences, my experience is that most talks

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:04.160
at most conferences are pretty terrible. So if you're going to spend all this time and

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.440
money bringing people together, why not spend all the extra and make sure they do a good

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.440
job of it?

00:03:09.479 --> 00:03:14.720
When you called me, I specifically remembered the sort of complication you were

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:22.039
experiencing, which was you needed to take your conference virtual because of COVID. And

00:03:22.039 --> 00:03:27.839
you also said something, you know, I, I'm fortunate in my life, I get to not pick and

00:03:27.839 --> 00:03:31.440
choose what I want to do. But you said something that really, really sparked me, which

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:38.559
was, I want to reach into people that normally wouldn't be chosen to speak, not just to

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:43.199
name people, but I want to give more people an opportunity to, you know, to give it a

00:03:43.679 --> 00:03:50.839
shot. And you knew that that would be risky for the conference. So you said, let's pair

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.919
the training. And that's where we started.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.080
That reminds me of the other thing that I learned from you that I think we put into

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:04.000
action pretty quickly was this idea. Instead of asking people to submit like abstracts

00:04:04.559 --> 00:04:11.679
to posit conf, we asked them to submit like a one minute video. And that is that is like

00:04:11.679 --> 00:04:16.959
it's such a good way of quickly telling is someone like genuinely passionate about this

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.200
topic. And then even if that's you and then like, as long as they're passionate, they

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.760
don't have to be like a great speaker because we know they're going to get that

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.079
training. Just made it so much easier.

00:04:27.799 --> 00:04:31.679
I have a favorite line I use and you'll hear some of them today, I'm sure, which was you

00:04:31.679 --> 00:04:37.320
can take a great idea and an average speaker and turn it into an awesome talk.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.119
But you can't take a great speaker with a crappy idea and turn it into an awesome talk.

00:04:43.119 --> 00:04:48.399
So if somebody is fearful or nervous or, you know, those things we can work with.

00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:53.559
But like coming with the right idea is 90 percent of the challenge.

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:59.079
And you and you can see that in the in the video one way or another.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:04.839
I don't I don't know if I ever talked about this with you, Ruth, but one, I almost made

00:05:04.839 --> 00:05:09.480
this a role that is if like if there is a musical instrument in the background of your

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:16.440
video, you must play it during it's like Chekhov's gun for for for talks and instruments.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:22.200
Yeah, we've had like a number of musical numbers that have been pretty amazing.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:27.320
So there was a I was just saying I was watching the submissions for the TEDx I'm working on

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.119
and there was a woman with a guitar like right here over her head.

00:05:30.119 --> 00:05:31.640
And I stared at it the whole time.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.399
Like, do you play to someone else?

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.760
I need I'm going to take that and steal that from you.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.480
Yeah, if there's something in the background, it's worth it.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.320
So that's how, Michael, that's how Hadley and I met was he got a little idea and we

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:46.040
took it from there.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:51.640
And I think your feedback over the years, I'll let you say, like how the conference

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:57.000
improved and actually how your filter really got more narrow about great talks.

00:05:57.799 --> 00:06:02.920
Yeah, it's interesting, too, because that's I think that was the first talk I spoken for

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:03.559
at Posit.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.559
First Posit conference I spoke at and kind of put me in touch with Posit in a lot of

00:06:09.559 --> 00:06:10.059
ways.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:12.920
It's interesting that was kind of my foot in the door.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:20.519
And I do think that filming a submission was really helpful versus kind of like really

00:06:21.559 --> 00:06:26.600
getting stuck on the page a little bit, though I know some people it goes the other way.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:31.640
Well, I'll also just say this, you know, with Wes in the room and all of you guys working

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:36.600
on AI things, you can't fake it when you have to submit a video.

00:06:38.119 --> 00:06:43.160
My son who's working, he's working on internship applications, was writing an essay yesterday,

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.839
and I might have tucked like a paragraph or two into AI to check something.

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:48.040
And he wrote me back on text.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.000
You know, he's in another room.

00:06:49.559 --> 00:06:53.160
Promise me you didn't use any AI to work on this.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.760
And I was like, I can't promise you.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:56.760
But we talked about it.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:00.760
And he is so anti, anti this at this point, which is great.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.239
He saw he saw some things happen at university that were troubling to him.

00:07:05.239 --> 00:07:08.600
He's like, I'm a human with really good ideas, and I can write these words.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.040
We want that in the submission.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:15.320
I know that that's not the case for everything, but we want your own ideas.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:19.239
But if we don't get it in writing, we can get it on the video.

00:07:19.239 --> 00:07:23.559
I mean, AI can definitely help with like clarifying and structuring or organizing your

00:07:23.559 --> 00:07:26.279
thinking, but it needs to be it needs to be your thoughts.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:31.640
And I think it's it's interesting how nowadays, like there seems to be a certain portion of the

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:39.480
population who are kind of hoping that that the thinking can be delegated to to the to

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:40.279
the LLMs.

00:07:40.279 --> 00:07:45.239
But we're finding over and over that, like, they, you know, they can't be trusted to think

00:07:45.239 --> 00:07:50.519
for us or to make decisions on our behalf, no matter how confident they seem or no matter

00:07:50.519 --> 00:07:54.359
how they they suck up to us and tell us that their ideas are good, because, of course,

00:07:54.359 --> 00:07:55.559
they think their ideas are good.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:01.239
But, you know, human judgment remains like for what I've been saying over and over again

00:08:01.239 --> 00:08:06.839
is that human judgment remains like the ultimate, you know, taste, taste making.

00:08:06.839 --> 00:08:08.359
So taste cannot be delegated.

00:08:08.359 --> 00:08:14.600
Nor can it be adjudicated on the you know, I think like, would you want AI defending

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:15.559
you on a stand?

00:08:15.559 --> 00:08:20.279
That's like, in my mind, you know, why the actuaries have to sign off on the risk and

00:08:20.279 --> 00:08:22.200
architects have to sign off on the plans.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.760
So yeah, but I do think there's a role for it.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:30.200
I'd be curious to hear a little bit more about I find it so interesting that you mentioned

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:36.440
like a person who struggles with speaking with a good idea, that idea that they can

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.400
kind of be trained and make it work.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:45.719
And I think you said something when we were preparing for this that that reminded me of,

00:08:45.719 --> 00:08:51.799
which is that data scientists are smart and they're working on compelling problems.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:55.880
But what you said there almost reminded me of that.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:02.840
Like, if you're a smart person and you have a compelling idea, like what what big challenges

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:07.559
do you see in that type of person, the sort of data scientist who's smart and compelling?

00:09:07.559 --> 00:09:10.039
What are they up against, do you think, when it comes to speaking?

00:09:10.599 --> 00:09:12.359
You know, I'm a big believer in three.

00:09:12.359 --> 00:09:14.119
So let's see if I can come up with three here.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:23.239
The first is recognizing that speaking is habitual, not natural, and it takes practice

00:09:23.239 --> 00:09:27.080
and they need to put in time and effort to get to that point where they really can feel

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:27.640
confident.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.960
It is not a switch on, switch off.

00:09:30.919 --> 00:09:32.039
We're not born speaking.

00:09:34.119 --> 00:09:37.880
We parent our parents at the beginning of life.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:43.320
And there's this, not to go into a tragic story, but there's this tragic story in Ohio

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:51.479
about 16 kids that were found, you know, in squalor and each they were ages 20 months

00:09:51.479 --> 00:09:58.039
to 18 years and none of them were verbal, which means that their parents never talked

00:09:58.039 --> 00:09:58.679
to them.

00:09:58.679 --> 00:10:01.640
If their parents had talked to them, they would have been able to articulate anything,

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:02.679
but they couldn't.

00:10:02.679 --> 00:10:05.559
So it just reminded us that, like, that's the first.

00:10:05.559 --> 00:10:07.239
The first is speaking is habitual.

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:08.280
More you do it, the more you get.

00:10:09.239 --> 00:10:14.679
The second is we're going to come, we're going to show up with everything that we've had

00:10:14.679 --> 00:10:17.000
in our history to that moment.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.479
And sometimes it's going to support us and sometimes it's not.

00:10:20.679 --> 00:10:26.599
And recognizing which of those habits might need to be sort of reimagined, which ones

00:10:26.599 --> 00:10:31.239
need to be created, which ones need to be left behind, whether it's the person with

00:10:31.239 --> 00:10:36.039
the ums or the uhs or the person who talks too long, all of those things.

00:10:36.039 --> 00:10:40.359
And so depending upon what the invitation is, you got to take a minute and say how,

00:10:40.359 --> 00:10:42.359
you know, I've only learned how to speak on panels.

00:10:42.359 --> 00:10:43.400
I've never done a keynote.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:45.159
How can I make that shift?

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:52.679
And the last one is you guys always have way too much to say in the time allotted.

00:10:54.359 --> 00:10:56.039
It's just the nature of your business.

00:10:56.919 --> 00:11:01.080
And the biggest challenge you always have is what's the most concise thing that you

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:01.719
can say?

00:11:01.719 --> 00:11:03.239
And Hadley's heard me say this.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:09.400
How much information do I have to share for you to believe something is true?

00:11:10.039 --> 00:11:16.119
Do I have to tell you every single, you know, do I have to show you 20 charts and graphs

00:11:16.119 --> 00:11:17.239
or can I show you two?

00:11:17.239 --> 00:11:22.039
Like, what is the question I'm answering for you that's on your mind or the question I

00:11:22.039 --> 00:11:22.760
want you to have?

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:25.640
And how can I get to that answer in the most concise way?

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:30.359
It doesn't mean that you can't click through and look at the appendix and go to the GitHub

00:11:30.359 --> 00:11:32.679
and all of the other resources that might exist.

00:11:33.719 --> 00:11:38.760
But the reality is we don't really remember most of what you say.

00:11:40.039 --> 00:11:43.799
So if you can say one really compelling thing and support it, then everyone's going to

00:11:43.799 --> 00:11:44.280
remember it.

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:45.799
So those would be my three.

00:11:45.799 --> 00:11:48.760
The first two are sort of related to habits.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:53.159
And the third is just knowing that the time and content is always going to be in a, it's

00:11:53.159 --> 00:11:54.760
always going to be a tension point.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.520
And the way you decide that is what were you invited to do?

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.799
And what's the question you're slated to ask?

00:12:01.799 --> 00:12:04.039
And how much time do you have in honoring that?

00:12:04.919 --> 00:12:08.039
So understanding sort of what the assignment is and what the question is you're trying

00:12:08.039 --> 00:12:12.679
to answer is half the battle, which is what we did when we coached for those five years.

00:12:12.679 --> 00:12:16.119
Every single call was what's the one thing you want to leave?

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:17.559
Very first call.

00:12:18.599 --> 00:12:20.039
What's the one idea you want to share?

00:12:20.679 --> 00:12:24.520
And iterating, iterating recursively on that.

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:29.960
And how do you find the right mix of the data and the story and the visuals to support

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:31.080
that one idea?

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:36.280
And if you go at it that way, let me share one really quick anecdote.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:42.520
There's a business in Columbus, a very large data-centered business that's been around

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:43.400
for 40 years.

00:12:44.039 --> 00:12:48.200
And they, I don't think they do it anymore, but they used to love, like two weeks of the

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:52.679
year, they'd send all their data scientists and marketing analysts and people on a deep

00:12:52.679 --> 00:12:53.159
dive.

00:12:53.159 --> 00:12:55.799
And they'd say, go find something interesting in the data.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.080
And this guy comes up to me at the end of it.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.479
I was doing a workshop on structured thinking.

00:13:01.479 --> 00:13:03.640
And he said, I don't know what they want.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:09.320
He was literally like, he's like, I have a 200-page deck and I have no idea what they're

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:09.799
looking for.

00:13:09.799 --> 00:13:13.239
And I said, did anyone give you a question to answer?

00:13:13.239 --> 00:13:15.559
And they just said, no, just go find something interesting.

00:13:15.559 --> 00:13:17.000
This is obviously before AI.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:21.080
And he was burned out because he kept looking and looking and looking.

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:26.359
And I thought, oh my, that's just like, that doesn't support good hierarchical thinking,

00:13:26.359 --> 00:13:29.799
which is putting the question that the audience wants answered at the top.

00:13:32.119 --> 00:13:35.960
When you're like, I say that's like diving into an ocean and saying, go find the baby

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:36.200
shark.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.119
I posted a baby shark on my LinkedIn this week.

00:13:38.119 --> 00:13:45.479
So go find the baby shark in the sea of, you know, you guys are smarter than to do that.

00:13:45.479 --> 00:13:47.880
But this is like 10 years ago.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.840
It reinforced for me how important that first step is.

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:59.479
You guys can put in the data for weeks and not get to anything to communicate at least.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:08.119
Yeah, I feel that too when speaking or sometimes prepping things and something that I have

00:14:08.119 --> 00:14:11.159
struggled with before where I just want to keep bringing points in.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:17.960
I'd be curious to hear, because I know the training, which I went through, goes through

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:18.919
a number of sessions.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:23.640
Maybe you could tell us just a little bit more about what that looks like, because it's

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:27.159
been such a key driver, I think, of PositConf.

00:14:27.159 --> 00:14:33.479
And it's actually wild for me to think about how many people you've coached.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:35.799
I actually never counted.

00:14:35.799 --> 00:14:41.880
But the last conference last year, Hadley, our team coached over 100 folks.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:45.880
And the year before, I mean, I think we started off with like 40 and then we went to like

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:46.440
60.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:51.320
And so it's, I don't know, we probably threw 400, I imagine.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.080
There are some repeats in there.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.200
And it's a few sessions.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:57.080
Is that right?

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:01.799
The way the coaching progresses is there's usually five sessions.

00:15:02.599 --> 00:15:05.559
And in York, in the Posit case, we did it in groups.

00:15:05.559 --> 00:15:08.359
So there was always a peer group to give you feedback.

00:15:08.359 --> 00:15:15.320
And we were always surprised how much people stayed in those calls and really worked with

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:15.799
each other.

00:15:15.799 --> 00:15:17.479
They just didn't show up for themselves.

00:15:17.479 --> 00:15:19.960
And that really speaks to the community you have.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:20.840
So that was the model.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:22.520
There were always five or six folks in there.

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:25.719
And the first call is always just, what's your idea?

00:15:25.719 --> 00:15:28.760
The second call is, get us to the kind of the top line outline.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:33.880
The third then starts to, because how do you pack it with the data you want to show in

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:34.520
the story?

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:36.440
Like that becomes a supporting character.

00:15:37.479 --> 00:15:41.080
And then there's the kind of the full talk to make sure it stays in time.

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:43.719
And then there's the, how do the visuals?

00:15:43.719 --> 00:15:50.039
And you're always writing out at least the finish of the whole talk before you finalize

00:15:50.039 --> 00:15:50.599
your visuals.

00:15:52.039 --> 00:15:57.880
I can't get my head around creating a deck and then trying to attach a talk to it.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:02.119
That's just, to me, contraindicator.

00:16:02.119 --> 00:16:04.359
I mean, it just doesn't work.

00:16:04.359 --> 00:16:11.960
Unless it is like an animation of something and you're talking me through a series.

00:16:12.039 --> 00:16:14.359
I did a cellular cancer talk once.

00:16:14.359 --> 00:16:16.119
And he's like, I have it in the animation.

00:16:16.119 --> 00:16:20.679
And I want the whole talk to be me walking through the four or five different turns of

00:16:20.679 --> 00:16:21.479
this animation.

00:16:21.479 --> 00:16:22.359
OK, that was different.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:28.520
And then there's usually a rehearsal where you're feeling confident.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:29.239
And guess what?

00:16:29.239 --> 00:16:33.159
Guess how many times you've shared the story and talked about it with others?

00:16:33.159 --> 00:16:35.479
At least five, sometimes more.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:37.719
And guess what?

00:16:37.719 --> 00:16:41.719
Then Hadley and his crew have a bunch of talks that are going to be on time.

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:46.200
Was a great idea and already vetted by peers.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.080
So it was brilliant.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:54.679
I mean, really, as we kind of co-created it together, the first rounds we did one on one,

00:16:54.679 --> 00:16:56.679
I think, no, we've always done groups.

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:59.159
But they got to know each other.

00:16:59.159 --> 00:17:05.640
And I was always just so surprised how attentive everyone was to wanting to be a peer coach.

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:08.520
I want the group so compelling because it's always.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:15.079
Like the things, the things that I kind of think are like maybe 80 percent of the value

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:20.199
of the training, which is not to like put down articulation, but like there's so much

00:17:20.199 --> 00:17:24.520
value in just like, hey, you have a deadline and you need to start thinking about your

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:25.800
talk the week before.

00:17:27.239 --> 00:17:32.760
And then and then you're going to show it to other people and listen to their feedback,

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:38.040
which is both great, like in terms of getting, oh, like no one understands this slide.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:42.760
But also you're like, OK, there's four other like now I've seen four other talks or five

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:44.680
other talks in this conference.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.800
And I have a better sense of like what worked, what didn't like that.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.119
It's just so like, I don't know.

00:17:50.119 --> 00:17:50.920
That's just so key.

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:55.079
Like, obviously, like the mentoring and the like the information about that, like how

00:17:55.079 --> 00:17:56.119
to give a talk is great.

00:17:56.119 --> 00:18:00.839
But I think that there's something so powerful about about that.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:01.420
Yeah.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:05.640
When we were in those calls, I mean, all we were really doing was looking for those things

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:09.160
that were going to get them like either make them fall off the stage or something where

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:10.920
people are going to say, I don't understand that.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:16.599
And we'd say, you know, put a comment in there, like explain a little bit more or throw that

00:18:16.599 --> 00:18:16.920
out.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:17.079
Right.

00:18:17.079 --> 00:18:19.400
Like just helping to give them license.

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:23.400
But you're also like modeling good coaching as well.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.719
And that's and then like that's people also picking that up and can then apply it when

00:18:27.719 --> 00:18:29.000
they listen to the other talks.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.439
But your point is exactly on that.

00:18:32.439 --> 00:18:37.800
We provide account all we really at the I mean, what the core of it is, is providing

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:42.599
a accountability partner that you will do the work more than three days before.

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:48.520
And you I mean, on the flip side, talks that are done three days before, you know it.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:53.000
Like there's this term called space learning.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.439
Maybe you guys have all heard it.

00:18:54.439 --> 00:18:57.800
Have you guys ever heard space learning where like you've got to you've got to like let

00:18:57.800 --> 00:19:00.040
it connect with your knowledge in your brain.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:04.599
You learn it one day and then you may come back to it two days later, which is why classes

00:19:04.599 --> 00:19:08.040
are designed in small bits, sort of can assemble.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:13.079
It's the same thing like it's space space practice where you sleep on it, you walk on

00:19:13.079 --> 00:19:15.160
it, you shower, you're like, oh, that didn't work again.

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:16.839
Oh, and somebody had an oh, right.

00:19:16.839 --> 00:19:20.920
Like and all of a sudden, the recursiveness of it, every time you encounter it, you get

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:21.479
better at it.

00:19:22.359 --> 00:19:24.199
And the more you can do that, the better the talk is.

00:19:24.199 --> 00:19:28.920
But if in three days you have no time for that iteration to happen.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:33.800
So, you know, and when you're like, hey, you know, when we when we invite people to

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:39.079
give a TEDx talk, there is on the application, will you show up to these coaching calls?

00:19:40.119 --> 00:19:41.719
And will you show up to rehearsal?

00:19:41.719 --> 00:19:45.160
And if they don't say yes, I mean, of course, they're going to say yes, but it's telling

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:48.119
them we value this immensely.

00:19:48.119 --> 00:19:50.199
So you can't really get out of it.

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:55.479
Hadley, I'm curious if you have a sense, because I know some of the motivation for

00:19:55.479 --> 00:20:02.680
this that that came up was to reach people you don't normally reach for PositConf and

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:06.119
to have them be prepared to give talks.

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:07.400
I'm curious.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:12.520
On your end, how how you've seen that heavy.

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:15.160
Do you feel like that happened?

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:19.560
And I'm curious what that how you kind of like saw that or what that looked like.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:21.160
If it did.

00:20:22.839 --> 00:20:27.479
I think so, like it's always it's hard to tell because there's like so many things

00:20:27.479 --> 00:20:33.719
that go into making a conference, but, you know, ensuring both the speakers and the audience

00:20:33.719 --> 00:20:39.880
were like diverse and reflected, you know, the broad community of people doing data science

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:42.119
like that's always been super important to us.

00:20:42.839 --> 00:20:45.239
And I think like the results show it.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:51.880
I think we, you know, we normally have like we end up like without the program committee

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:57.800
having to put their thumb on the scales, like we end up with like 50 50 women and men.

00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:05.560
We end up with like, you know, all the different races represented, not just like the white

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:10.280
people and Asian people you see in like the majority of tech conferences.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:12.040
So I think it worked.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:17.800
I think that helped like the fact that you're putting together a video, which is a little

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:23.880
intimidating for people who are like very established kind of speakers who, you know,

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:27.640
have an existing kind of portfolio of abstracts they could just copy and paste in.

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:31.400
Like it's kind of a it levels the playing field a bit.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:35.479
And then then because we know that there's going to have this training, we can pick people

00:21:35.479 --> 00:21:40.839
who are like newer voices that we don't, you know, never met them before.

00:21:40.839 --> 00:21:43.640
We don't know anything about their background, but they've got a great idea.

00:21:44.359 --> 00:21:46.599
I think it's been really that's been really effective.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:51.319
And I suspect people are more willing to submit to like if they know that they're going to

00:21:51.319 --> 00:21:57.160
get some training, like it's less intimidating than this being like your first ever talk.

00:21:57.160 --> 00:21:59.719
And you just like throw it on the deep end.

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:01.400
And that was when you called.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:02.199
You're like, I want to.

00:22:04.119 --> 00:22:04.920
You're taking a risk.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:07.719
You were taking a risk on the virtual and then you were like, I don't want to take a

00:22:07.719 --> 00:22:15.160
double risk on not only having sort of known people, but the reason that I got introduced

